254
00:15:59,610 --> 00:16:07,030
Another powerful one is to maintain focus and efficiency, I must tackle my tasks in order of agreed company priority.

255
00:16:07,250 --> 00:16:11,880
So how does anchoring your refusal and agreed upon priorities make it less personal and more objective?

256
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:14,630
And why is that so critical for the overall mission?

257
00:16:14,610 --> 00:16:24,130
This type of declination is incredibly powerful precisely because it anchors your refusal in objective, agreed upon priorities or broader strategic goals.

258
00:16:24,810 --> 00:16:30,160
That makes it far less personal and much more difficult to, you know, argue with.

259
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:30,490
Right.

260
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:31,590
It's not just your opinion.

261
00:16:31,830 --> 00:16:32,390
Exactly.

262
00:16:32,430 --> 00:16:32,790
Yeah.

263
00:16:32,770 --> 00:16:41,880
When you say, while I see the value in this idea, it's not aligned with the strategic priorities set for this quarter, you're not saying the idea itself is bad or that you're unwilling.

264
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:47,150
You're explaining that now is not the time for you to take it on, given the larger predefined objectives.

265
00:16:47,170 --> 00:16:48,110
Context is key.

266
00:16:48,090 --> 00:16:48,410
Yes.

267
00:16:49,030 --> 00:16:55,160
This reinforces your commitment to shared objectives and helps the requester understand the broader context that informs your decision.

268
00:16:55,520 --> 00:17:02,710
Imagine a situation where a colleague suggests a really interesting side project, but it pulls resources away from the company's main growth initiative.

269
00:17:02,730 --> 00:17:03,470
