928
00:57:38,460 --> 00:57:44,030
And the result is always, well, it's usually a messy project and a lot of wasted time for everyone involved.

929
00:57:44,110 --> 00:57:50,460
It's actually a sign of maturity, I think, and good judgment to be able to say, you know what, I'm probably not the best person for this.

930
00:57:50,580 --> 00:57:54,160
Rather than taking it on, struggling, and potentially delivering poor results.

931
00:57:54,280 --> 00:57:54,860
Exactly.

932
00:57:55,210 --> 00:58:02,700
It conserves your resources, protects your reputation for quality, and makes sure the work actually gets done correctly by someone who knows how.

933
00:58:03,120 --> 00:58:09,410
Okay, so let's dive into some specific phrases for when it's not your domain, starting with maybe a direct but helpful one.

934
00:58:09,680 --> 00:58:10,420
Okay, how about this?

935
00:58:11,260 --> 00:58:13,170
This falls outside my current expertise.

936
00:58:13,190 --> 00:58:13,470
Short.

937
00:58:13,670 --> 00:58:13,970
Simple.

938
00:58:14,470 --> 00:58:14,790
Very.

939
00:58:15,490 --> 00:58:19,060
It's a clear, concise, and pretty objective reason for declining.

940
00:58:19,940 --> 00:58:24,130
It states that the task is simply beyond your specific knowledge or skill set.

941
00:58:24,410 --> 00:58:26,860
Without needing a long apology or explanation.

942
00:58:27,050 --> 00:58:27,260
Right.

943
00:58:27,480 --> 00:58:29,100
It's professional and straight to the point.

944
00:58:29,720 --> 00:58:36,290
And it implicitly suggests that someone else would probably be a better fit without you necessarily having to name names right away.

945
00:58:36,450 --> 00:58:36,670
Yeah.

946
00:58:37,030 --> 00:58:37,810
Wonderfully efficient.

947
00:58:38,150 --> 00:58:43,160
For those quick, direct refusals where it's obvious it's not your area, gets the job done without fuss.

948
00:58:43,320 --> 00:58:43,580
Okay.

949
00:58:43,720 --> 00:58:49,620
A slightly softer alternative, which also feels very collaborative, is I may not be the best person for this.

950
00:58:50,410 --> 00:58:51,550
Ah, may not be the best.

951
00:58:52,190 --> 00:58:52,850
I like that.

952
00:58:52,980 --> 00:58:53,480
Yeah.

953
00:58:53,500 --> 00:58:58,870
By saying, I may not be the best person, you're subtly implying that someone else is actually better suited.

954
00:58:59,670 --> 00:59:05,760
It feels less about your inability and more about optimizing for the best possible outcome for the task itself.

955
00:59:06,000 --> 00:59:06,240
Right.

956
00:59:06,400 --> 00:59:10,660
And it sets you up perfectly to follow up with a helpful redirection, doesn't it?

957
00:59:10,890 --> 00:59:11,390
Exactly.

958
00:59:11,410 --> 00:59:14,650
You could follow with, perhaps Sarah Miller from marketing could assist with this.

959
00:59:14,930 --> 00:59:16,010
We're not just saying no.

960
00:59:16,690 --> 00:59:18,570
You're facilitating a more effective outcome.

961
00:59:19,020 --> 00:59:22,380
Which leaves a positive impression, even though you declined.

962
00:59:22,460 --> 00:59:22,780
Right.

963
00:59:23,180 --> 00:59:28,890
Now, another one that's a bit more formal and focuses more on, say, organizational structure.

964
00:59:29,810 --> 00:59:32,800
This particular responsibility is usually handled by another department.

965
00:59:33,260 --> 00:59:35,160
OK, so that shifts away from you personally.

966
00:59:35,220 --> 00:59:35,640
Immediately.

967
00:59:36,320 --> 00:59:39,110
It removes any personal responsibility from the decline.

968
00:59:39,130 --> 00:59:40,750
It's presented as a systemic reason.

969
00:59:41,170 --> 00:59:42,230
You know, this is how we're set up.

970
00:59:42,630 --> 00:59:43,530
Not a personal choice.

971
00:59:44,040 --> 00:59:47,860
And it helps the requester understand the proper channels for their request.

972
00:59:47,910 --> 00:59:52,320
Without you having to take on the burden of finding the right person or process for them.

973
00:59:52,860 --> 00:59:53,400
Exactly.

974
00:59:53,840 --> 00:59:58,060
So it's not, I don't want to, it's, this is simply not how our organization functions.

975
00:59:58,800 --> 01:00:03,090
Very clear, very efficient for requests that clearly cross departmental lines.

976
01:00:03,110 --> 01:00:03,390
Okay.

977
01:00:03,990 --> 01:00:08,300
And sometimes it's not a whole department, but maybe just a really specific skill you don't have.

978
01:00:08,580 --> 01:00:08,720
Right.

979
01:00:08,740 --> 01:00:09,920
Maybe it's the niche skill set.

980
01:00:10,260 --> 01:00:15,910
In that case, you could say something like, my knowledge on this specific subject is somewhat limited, I'm afraid.

981
01:00:16,160 --> 01:00:17,000
Yeah, somewhat limited.

982
01:00:17,020 --> 01:00:19,260
That feels honest, humble almost.

983
01:00:19,520 --> 01:00:19,860
It does.

984
01:00:20,320 --> 01:00:21,850
It's an honest and humble approach.

985
01:00:22,590 --> 01:00:27,400
The somewhat limited part indicates you might have a basic understanding perhaps.

986
01:00:28,160 --> 01:00:33,730
But crucially, not enough to confidently take the lead or deliver a high quality outcome.

987
01:00:33,710 --> 01:00:36,890
So you're preventing yourself from doing them a disservice, really.

988
01:00:36,910 --> 01:00:37,490
Precisely.

989
01:00:37,740 --> 01:00:38,620
That's a great way to put it.

990
01:00:38,960 --> 01:00:40,740
It manages expectations effectively.

991
01:00:41,000 --> 01:00:49,250
You're not feigning complete ignorance, but you're acknowledging that while you might know a little, you don't know enough to be the primary resource if they want a quality result.

992
01:00:49,490 --> 01:00:49,890
Got it.

993
01:00:50,480 --> 01:00:53,500
What about a stronger version of that, really emphasizing quality?

994
01:00:53,540 --> 01:00:54,380
Okay.

995
01:00:54,400 --> 01:00:58,670
For a strong statement that highlights your commitment to quality, even when declining.

996
01:00:59,630 --> 01:01:04,600
I wouldn't want to provide suboptimal support as this is outside my core competencies.

997
01:01:04,620 --> 01:01:07,860
Ooh, suboptimal support and core competencies.

998
01:01:08,300 --> 01:01:09,510
Very professional terms.

999
01:01:09,630 --> 01:01:09,970
They are.

1000
01:01:10,010 --> 01:01:12,170
They emphasize quality and skill alignment.

1001
01:01:12,190 --> 01:01:16,980
You're essentially saying, look, I can try, but it wouldn't be good because it's not what I do best.

1002
01:01:17,480 --> 01:01:21,830
And frankly, I refuse to deliver anything less than excellent work in my name.

1003
01:01:22,150 --> 01:01:23,070
Wow.

1004
01:01:23,300 --> 01:01:25,750
That really shows a strong commitment to quality, doesn't it?

1005
01:01:25,930 --> 01:01:27,830
Even if it means declining the task.

1006
01:01:27,850 --> 01:01:28,790
It absolutely does.

1007
01:01:29,230 --> 01:01:32,090
That phrase speaks volumes about your professional standards.

1008
01:01:32,370 --> 01:01:35,800
It turns a no into a statement about your commitment to excellence.

1009
01:01:35,820 --> 01:01:37,580
Which is certainly a powerful way to frame it.

1010
01:01:38,160 --> 01:01:40,920
Okay, so we've covered capacity and expertise.

1011
01:01:41,060 --> 01:01:41,580
What's next?

1012
01:01:41,860 --> 01:01:43,910
What other big categories lead to a no?

1013
01:01:44,390 --> 01:01:50,810
Well, sometimes it's not about you or your skills directly, but about the practicalities, the real-world constraints.

1014
01:01:50,840 --> 01:01:51,700
Ah, right.

1015
01:01:51,780 --> 01:01:55,090
The stuff that just makes things impossible, even if you want to help.

1016
01:01:55,110 --> 01:01:55,750
Exactly.

1017
01:01:56,170 --> 01:01:57,110
So what does this all mean?

1018
01:01:58,010 --> 01:02:04,020
It means even the best ideas or the most well-intentioned requests can sometimes just hit a wall.

1019
01:02:04,160 --> 01:02:04,420
Yeah.

1020
01:02:04,720 --> 01:02:16,460
When faced with the cold, hard realities of resources, whether that's money, people, or just plain logistics, sometimes, despite your desire to help, a request is simply unfeasible.

1021
01:02:16,680 --> 01:02:19,930
Because of budget constraints, maybe limited team capacity.

1022
01:02:20,050 --> 01:02:20,270
Right.

1023
01:02:20,550 --> 01:02:22,310
Or just practical impossibilities.

1024
01:02:22,530 --> 01:02:23,990
And these aren't personal shortcomings.

1025
01:02:24,010 --> 01:02:27,940
They're objective realities that just need to be communicated clearly and professionally.

1026
01:02:28,040 --> 01:02:28,660
Okay.

1027
01:02:28,680 --> 01:02:34,740
So recognizing and communicating these limitations, that's a crucial part of responsible management and project planning.

1028
01:02:34,720 --> 01:02:35,340
Absolutely.

1029
01:02:35,580 --> 01:02:36,800
It helps prevent overcommitment.

1030
01:02:36,820 --> 01:02:37,860
It helps prevent burnout.

1031
01:02:38,200 --> 01:02:40,580
And ultimately, it prevents project failures down the line.

1032
01:02:40,660 --> 01:02:41,120
Right.

1033
01:02:41,140 --> 01:02:46,670
These phrases allow you to be transparent about the constraints, but without sounding negative or uncooperative.

1034
01:02:46,690 --> 01:02:50,190
It's about being pragmatic and prioritizing what is actually achievable.

1035
01:02:50,310 --> 01:02:50,550
Okay.

1036
01:02:50,710 --> 01:02:54,600
Let's start with phrases for just questioning the feasibility or practicality.

1037
01:02:55,120 --> 01:03:00,020
These often act as a kind of implicit no, right, by inviting a reality check.

1038
01:03:00,260 --> 01:03:00,680
Exactly.

1039
01:03:01,160 --> 01:03:04,720
They open the door for discussion rather than shutting it immediately.

1040
01:03:04,700 --> 01:03:08,170
First up, a simple but effective question.

1041
01:03:09,370 --> 01:03:11,920
Is this achievable in the given time frame?

1042
01:03:12,160 --> 01:03:12,900
Straightforward.

1043
01:03:13,120 --> 01:03:14,260
Flags the time issue.

1044
01:03:14,440 --> 01:03:14,640
Right.

1045
01:03:14,880 --> 01:03:19,970
It's an open question, immediately flags potential issues with the timeline, but without outright refusing.

1046
01:03:20,830 --> 01:03:24,220
It cleverly shifts the burden of proof back to the requester.

1047
01:03:24,570 --> 01:03:44,910
asking them to consider the practicalities more deeply yeah you're not saying no you're saying help me understand how this could be a yes so you'd use this when you suspect the timeline is just well unrealistic gives them a chance to reconsider or maybe explain how they see it working precisely or if you have broader concerns not just about time maybe logistics or something else

1048
01:03:45,200 --> 01:03:46,970
I have some concerns about its practicality.

1049
01:03:47,010 --> 01:03:47,770
Practicality.

1050
01:03:47,890 --> 01:03:48,870
That's quite broad.

1051
01:03:48,950 --> 01:03:49,190
It is.

1052
01:03:49,510 --> 01:03:50,890
And that's its strength sometimes.

1053
01:03:51,310 --> 01:03:56,540
It's a broad term that invites discussion without you having to pinpoint every single issue right away.

1054
01:03:56,920 --> 01:04:02,760
It suggests there might be various logistical hurdles or real-world challenges that maybe haven't been fully considered yet.

1055
01:04:03,020 --> 01:04:03,840
Yeah, I like that.

1056
01:04:03,910 --> 01:04:08,650
It's vague enough to open a dialogue without you having to list every single potential pitfall immediately.

1057
01:04:08,670 --> 01:04:10,830
It just says, hmm, let's talk this through.

1058
01:04:10,850 --> 01:04:15,080
Okay, moving to something a bit more specific, maybe focusing on resources.

1059
01:04:15,680 --> 01:04:16,800
This seems quite ambitious.

1060
01:04:17,300 --> 01:04:20,390
Are all necessary resources available for the launch?

1061
01:04:21,050 --> 01:04:21,750
Ambitious.

1062
01:04:22,640 --> 01:04:24,900
That's a polite word for maybe unrealistic.

1063
01:04:25,100 --> 01:04:25,860
It can be, yes.

1064
01:04:26,500 --> 01:04:32,980
It's a polite way to suggest something might be overly optimistic or perhaps under-resourced.

1065
01:04:33,000 --> 01:04:36,620
And following it with a direct question about necessary resources.

1066
01:04:36,660 --> 01:04:37,390
Yeah.

1067
01:04:37,600 --> 01:04:44,630
immediately zeroes in on a key potential barrier, whether that's money, people, equipment, whatever it might be.

1068
01:04:44,770 --> 01:04:49,470
This is great when you're unsure about the match between the scope of the project and the actual resources available.

1069
01:04:49,610 --> 01:04:52,260
It invites that reality check without being confrontational.

1070
01:04:52,420 --> 01:04:52,860
Exactly.

1071
01:04:53,000 --> 01:04:58,200
And if you're looking at a really large-scale strategic decision, something with many unknowns, you might say.

1072
01:04:58,940 --> 01:05:03,090
I'm wondering if we've fully considered all the implications of this merger, for example.

1073
01:05:03,110 --> 01:05:04,650
Okay, that signals a need for caution.

1074
01:05:04,870 --> 01:05:05,450
Deeper thought.

1075
01:05:05,610 --> 01:05:05,920
Right.

1076
01:05:06,880 --> 01:05:12,830
It indicates a need for deeper strategic analysis before proceeding with a major decision like that.

1077
01:05:13,410 --> 01:05:23,130
It subtly implies that there might be unforeseen consequences or complexities, financial, operational, cultural, personal, that haven't been adequately addressed yet.

1078
01:05:23,380 --> 01:05:29,350
It's like saying, well, let's pump the brakes just for a second here, because this could have ripple effects we haven't even dreamed of yet.

1079
01:05:29,690 --> 01:05:31,800
A smart move for those really big decisions.

1080
01:05:32,030 --> 01:05:32,430
Definitely.

1081
01:05:32,760 --> 01:05:39,820
And one more in this category, a phrase that acts as a strong no, but cleverly suggests a path forward if the parameters can shift.

1082
01:05:40,280 --> 01:05:45,250
To deliver by next Monday, we might need to reduce the report scope or add temporary resources.

1083
01:05:45,690 --> 01:05:46,950
Ah, the tradeoff phrase.

1084
01:05:47,030 --> 01:05:47,530
Exactly.

1085
01:05:48,070 --> 01:05:51,940
This clearly states the tradeoffs required to meet an aggressive deadline like that.

1086
01:05:51,920 --> 01:05:55,520
It implies the current request, as it stands, is unfeasible.

1087
01:05:56,070 --> 01:05:58,090
But it offers concrete ways to make it work.

1088
01:05:58,330 --> 01:06:00,850
So it shifts the burden back to the requester, doesn't it?

1089
01:06:00,870 --> 01:06:03,930
Like, what's more important to you, the deadline or the full scope?

1090
01:06:04,240 --> 01:06:04,700
Precisely.

1091
01:06:05,280 --> 01:06:09,700
That's powerful because it shows you're not just saying no, you're actively problem solving.

1092
01:06:10,200 --> 01:06:13,950
It's kind of a conditional yes disguised as a strong no.

1093
01:06:14,710 --> 01:06:17,670
And it puts the ball right back in their court to prioritize.

1094
01:06:17,710 --> 01:06:18,310
Very useful.

1095
01:06:18,690 --> 01:06:19,770
Okay, let's tackle the big one.

1096
01:06:20,680 --> 01:06:21,880
Budgetary constraints.

1097
01:06:23,140 --> 01:06:25,020
It's often the elephant in the room, right?

1098
01:06:25,040 --> 01:06:25,730
It really can be.

1099
01:06:25,890 --> 01:06:31,440
How do you say no because of money without sounding like you're just being, well, cheap or uncooperative?

1100
01:06:31,560 --> 01:06:33,000
Yeah, that's tricky.

1101
01:06:33,400 --> 01:06:37,990
But budget is often a very objective, sometimes non-negotiable constraint.

1102
01:06:38,510 --> 01:06:42,070
So these phrases work by depersonalizing the refusal.

1103
01:06:42,310 --> 01:06:42,770
Okay.

1104
01:06:42,790 --> 01:06:45,960
They point to financial realities or establish financial processes.

1105
01:06:46,320 --> 01:06:49,720
It makes clear that it's a systemic limitation, not your personal choice.

1106
01:06:49,760 --> 01:06:52,190
It's about responsible financial stewardship, really.

1107
01:06:52,350 --> 01:06:52,890
Got it.

1108
01:06:52,950 --> 01:06:54,530
So starting with a very direct one.

1109
01:06:54,640 --> 01:06:57,970
How about, we lack the budget for this new software now, for instance.

1110
01:06:58,190 --> 01:06:58,530
Direct.

1111
01:06:58,830 --> 01:06:59,090
Clear.

1112
01:06:59,430 --> 01:06:59,790
Very.

1113
01:07:00,390 --> 01:07:02,480
It states a current financial impossibility.

1114
01:07:02,920 --> 01:07:04,580
The now component is key, though.

1115
01:07:05,180 --> 01:07:09,370
It hints that maybe it could be possible later, but right now the funds simply aren't available.

1116
01:07:09,690 --> 01:07:10,970
Concise, to the point.

1117
01:07:11,270 --> 01:07:11,650
Simple.

1118
01:07:12,110 --> 01:07:12,610
No fuss.

1119
01:07:12,930 --> 01:07:14,740
Gets the message across immediately.

1120
01:07:15,160 --> 01:07:21,210
Or, if you want to explain why it's not possible budget-wise, this training isn't currently factored into our budget.

1121
01:07:21,190 --> 01:07:22,070
Factored into.

1122
01:07:22,290 --> 01:07:23,830
OK, so that implies planning.

1123
01:07:24,030 --> 01:07:24,510
Exactly.

1124
01:07:24,790 --> 01:07:26,580
It means it wasn't planned for financially.

1125
01:07:27,320 --> 01:07:32,140
This implies that the current budget doesn't have a specific allocation for this expenditure.

1126
01:07:32,950 --> 01:07:37,330
It makes it a matter of foresight and planning, not just the general lack of funds right now.

1127
01:07:37,350 --> 01:07:37,610
Right.

1128
01:07:37,650 --> 01:07:43,320
So it's less about being stingy and more about, look, this just wasn't on our financial roadmap for this period.

1129
01:07:43,540 --> 01:07:44,380
That's a good distinction.

1130
01:07:44,660 --> 01:07:48,650
And for situations involving formal processes, you might say.

1131
01:07:49,320 --> 01:07:52,640
I'm afraid we don't have the necessary funds approved for such an initiative.

1132
01:07:53,160 --> 01:07:53,960
Approved funds.

1133
01:07:55,120 --> 01:07:56,290
That points to a process.

1134
01:07:56,550 --> 01:07:56,870
Definitely.

1135
01:07:57,110 --> 01:08:01,670
It implies a formal process that either hasn't happened or hasn't been completed successfully.

1136
01:08:02,130 --> 01:08:10,880
This removes the personal responsibility for the lack of funds and points squarely to a procedural barrier, maybe a bureaucratic reality that's out of your hands.

1137
01:08:11,260 --> 01:08:18,890
That's a very clear and defensible reason for declining, especially when dealing with requests that need that formal budget sign-off.

1138
01:08:19,070 --> 01:08:19,710
Absolutely.

1139
01:08:19,730 --> 01:08:29,580
And for something that goes beyond a specific budgeted line item, the requested expenditure exceeds what we have currently earmarked for marketing, let's say.

1140
01:08:30,080 --> 01:08:30,600
Earmarked.

1141
01:08:30,760 --> 01:08:32,790
So that means money set aside specifically.

1142
01:08:32,910 --> 01:08:33,570
Yes, exactly.

1143
01:08:34,050 --> 01:08:39,720
Earmarked indicates specific funds that have been set aside for a particular purpose, like a marketing campaign budget.

1144
01:08:40,380 --> 01:08:45,160
This phrase is used when a request goes over that specific departmental or project budget.

1145
01:08:45,680 --> 01:08:51,490
It signaled a clear boundary that can't be crossed without significant reevaluation or finding funds from elsewhere.

1146
01:08:51,950 --> 01:08:59,300
So it's not just a general no money, it's a no because we have a very specific pot of money allocated for this and your request is bigger than that pot.

1147
01:08:59,640 --> 01:09:00,240
Very clear.

1148
01:09:00,800 --> 01:09:01,260
Very clear.

1149
01:09:02,000 --> 01:09:06,080
And finally, one that offers a path to compromise, focusing on the budget issue.

1150
01:09:06,540 --> 01:09:11,990
Could we explore lower cost software alternatives that fit within our current budget parameters?

1151
01:09:12,390 --> 01:09:13,810
Ah, offering a solution again.

1152
01:09:14,110 --> 01:09:14,710
Yes.

1153
01:09:15,310 --> 01:09:18,600
It offers a collaborative solution by looking for cheaper options.

1154
01:09:19,360 --> 01:09:25,830
It shows you're willing to find a way forward, even if the original specific request is unfeasible financially.

1155
01:09:26,550 --> 01:09:30,240
It focuses on solutions within the existing financial constraints.

1156
01:09:30,740 --> 01:09:31,740
That feels like a win-win.

1157
01:09:31,760 --> 01:09:35,760
You're saying no to the specific high cost, but yes to finding a solution that works.

1158
01:09:36,040 --> 01:09:38,470
It's problem solving, not just gatekeeping the funds.

1159
01:09:38,750 --> 01:09:39,230
Exactly.

1160
01:09:39,890 --> 01:09:40,730
Maintains goodwill.

1161
01:09:40,830 --> 01:09:41,290
OK.

1162
01:09:41,710 --> 01:09:45,800
So sometimes, though, it's not about your personal capacity or even the budget.

1163
01:09:46,020 --> 01:09:48,280
It's about the entire team's workload.

1164
01:09:48,320 --> 01:09:48,520
Right.

1165
01:09:48,640 --> 01:09:48,760
Right.

1166
01:09:48,780 --> 01:09:50,020
Or maybe even their well-being.

1167
01:09:50,200 --> 01:09:50,620
Absolutely.

1168
01:09:50,900 --> 01:09:58,620
How do you decline a request on behalf of your team without sounding like you're just, you know, shirking responsibility or complaining about how busy everyone is?

1169
01:09:58,720 --> 01:10:01,320
That's a really important one for managers and team leads.

1170
01:10:01,740 --> 01:10:06,330
It's about protecting your team's focus, their productivity, and frankly, their morale.

1171
01:10:06,650 --> 01:10:06,850
Right.

1172
01:10:07,170 --> 01:10:17,140
These phrases convey that your refusal isn't just saying no to more work, but it's actually a strategic decision, a decision made to ensure the team's overall success and prevent burnout.

1173
01:10:17,280 --> 01:10:17,520
Okay.

1174
01:10:17,810 --> 01:10:19,310
So it shows responsible leadership.

1175
01:10:19,470 --> 01:10:19,750
Yes.

1176
01:10:20,260 --> 01:10:24,160
And a commitment to sustainable output, not just burning people out.

1177
01:10:24,390 --> 01:10:24,730
Got it.

1178
01:10:25,130 --> 01:10:28,460
So let's start with maybe a general team capacity statement.

1179
01:10:28,780 --> 01:10:29,260
Okay.

1180
01:10:29,280 --> 01:10:29,900
Simple and clear.

1181
01:10:30,600 --> 01:10:32,990
My engineering team is at full capacity this month.

1182
01:10:33,770 --> 01:10:35,900
Using the team name makes it specific.

1183
01:10:35,990 --> 01:10:36,390
Right.

1184
01:10:36,630 --> 01:10:40,080
Broadens that full capacity idea we discussed earlier to the whole team.

1185
01:10:40,260 --> 01:10:40,660
Exactly.

1186
01:10:40,740 --> 01:10:51,590
It's a clear objective statement that the entire team is maxed out and simply cannot take on more work in this specific period, say this month or this quarter.

1187
01:10:51,570 --> 01:10:55,360
It communicates a collective reality, not just your personal situation.

1188
01:10:55,440 --> 01:10:58,920
So it's not just I'm busy, it's we as a unit are busy.

1189
01:10:59,380 --> 01:11:01,170
Very direct, very team focused.

1190
01:11:01,190 --> 01:11:05,270
And if that busyness is actually threatening existing commitments, you could be more specific.

1191
01:11:06,010 --> 01:11:11,560
Adding this new client project would overburden the team and risk current deadlines for Project Titan, for example.

1192
01:11:12,020 --> 01:11:14,910
Ooh, connecting it to specific negative consequences.

1193
01:11:15,040 --> 01:11:18,950
Yes, this clearly articulates the potential negative outcomes.

1194
01:11:19,810 --> 01:11:30,270
Overburden, which implies stress, reduced quality, inefficiency, and risk current deadlines, which impacts existing potentially critical projects like Project Titan.

1195
01:11:31,430 --> 01:11:34,770
It presents a very tangible downside to accepting the new request.

1196
01:11:34,920 --> 01:11:36,850
Yeah, that's highlighting a critical trade-off.

1197
01:11:36,890 --> 01:11:44,150
You're effectively saying, look, if we say yes to this new thing, we're implicitly saying no or risking failure on something else that's already vital.

1198
01:11:44,510 --> 01:11:45,250
Very effective.

1199
01:11:45,420 --> 01:11:47,280
It makes the choice clear for the person asking.

1200
01:11:47,980 --> 01:11:52,110
And for a more empathetic approach, focusing on team health, which is increasingly important.

1201
01:11:52,130 --> 01:11:52,850
Yeah, definitely.

1202
01:11:52,870 --> 01:11:58,070
We'd say, I must consider the team's overall well-being and prevent collective burnout by saying no now.

1203
01:11:58,380 --> 01:11:58,680
Wow.

1204
01:11:59,320 --> 01:12:00,940
That's quite direct about well-being.

1205
01:12:01,220 --> 01:12:02,620
It is, and sometimes necessary.

1206
01:12:03,200 --> 01:12:08,270
It highlights the human element, focusing squarely on preventing burnout and maintaining team morale.

1207
01:12:08,250 --> 01:12:12,500
This is a very empathetic and I'd argue very responsible reason to decline.

1208
01:12:13,200 --> 01:12:19,760
It prioritizes the team's long-term health and sustainability over maybe just short-term gains or accommodating one more request.

1209
01:12:20,010 --> 01:12:20,810
That's powerful.

1210
01:12:20,890 --> 01:12:25,520
It really shows you're a leader who cares about their people, not just the output figures.

1211
01:12:26,000 --> 01:12:29,480
It's a no that could actually foster trust and loyalty in the long run.

1212
01:12:29,600 --> 01:12:30,160
I believe so.

1213
01:12:30,600 --> 01:12:35,030
And sometimes you need to protect your team from that constant reactive cycle, right?

1214
01:12:35,210 --> 01:12:36,810
The endless urgent tasks.

1215
01:12:37,030 --> 01:12:37,430
Oh, yeah.

1216
01:12:38,010 --> 01:12:39,150
That kills productivity.

1217
01:12:39,270 --> 01:12:39,650
It does.

1218
01:12:40,570 --> 01:12:47,160
So you might say, I'm committed to sustainable work pacing for my creative team, so I can't accept this urgent task.

1219
01:12:47,180 --> 01:12:49,010
Mentioning the team type adds context.

1220
01:12:49,150 --> 01:12:49,570
Okay.

1221
01:12:49,600 --> 01:12:50,800
Sustainable work pacing.

1222
01:12:51,020 --> 01:12:51,820
I like that phrase.

1223
01:12:52,080 --> 01:12:54,700
It highlights a commitment to a healthy, consistent work pace.

1224
01:12:55,660 --> 01:13:01,370
It explicitly rejects unsustainable, urgent demands that often lead to burnout and lower quality work eventually.

1225
01:13:01,970 --> 01:13:07,500
It sets a boundary against constantly reacting to emergencies, which helps foster better planning and long-term productivity.

1226
01:13:07,720 --> 01:13:13,210
So it's about breaking that cycle of constant firefighting and prioritizing quality work and mental health.

1227
01:13:13,610 --> 01:13:14,070
That's a no.

1228
01:13:14,170 --> 01:13:15,570
That definitely pays dividends later.

1229
01:13:15,990 --> 01:13:16,430
Absolutely.

1230
01:13:16,950 --> 01:13:22,020
And finally, one that gracefully redirects the request, aiming for the best outcome overall.

1231
01:13:22,940 --> 01:13:29,150
Could this research request be handled by another department or perhaps another team with more available bandwidth right now?

1232
01:13:29,130 --> 01:13:31,360
Ah, helpful redirection.

1233
01:13:31,400 --> 01:13:31,600
Right.

1234
01:13:32,100 --> 01:13:40,680
It offers a potential solution by suggesting an alternative team that might be better suited, maybe skill-wise, or perhaps just less busy at the moment.

1235
01:13:41,120 --> 01:13:45,450
It shows you're willing to help find a solution, even if your own team can't take it on directly.

1236
01:13:45,530 --> 01:13:47,840
Without overcommitting your already stretched team.

1237
01:13:47,960 --> 01:13:48,420
Exactly.

1238
01:13:48,440 --> 01:13:49,340
It's a classic, right?

1239
01:13:49,940 --> 01:13:52,390
It's not really for us, but I know someone who might be perfect.

1240
01:13:52,400 --> 01:13:52,610
Yeah.

1241
01:13:53,110 --> 01:13:57,210
It demonstrates helpfulness even while declining the direct ask.

1242
01:13:57,230 --> 01:13:57,770
Great stuff.

1243
01:13:57,900 --> 01:13:58,680
Okay, moving on.

1244
01:13:59,000 --> 01:14:00,900
Here's where it gets really interesting, I think.

1245
01:14:01,360 --> 01:14:06,450
Sometimes a no isn't about your capacity or your expertise or even the team's bandwidth.

1246
01:14:06,870 --> 01:14:07,590
It's about...

1247
01:14:08,420 --> 01:14:09,120
the rules.

1248
01:14:09,580 --> 01:14:09,940
Ah, yes.

1249
01:14:10,280 --> 01:14:11,880
Policy, compliance, ethics.

1250
01:14:12,020 --> 01:14:12,500
Exactly.

1251
01:14:12,620 --> 01:14:16,170
It's about company policy, legal compliance, maybe ethical guidelines.

1252
01:14:16,350 --> 01:14:20,040
And this kind of note, it really takes the personal aspect out of the decline, doesn't it?

1253
01:14:20,320 --> 01:14:29,940
It makes it about a non-negotiable standard, something that protects not just you, but the entire organization from legal risks, financial trouble, reputational damage.

1254
01:14:29,910 --> 01:14:30,560
Absolutely.

1255
01:14:30,700 --> 01:14:38,790
When a request bumps up against organizational rules, legal requirements or just established procedures, a no becomes a necessary safeguard.

1256
01:14:38,990 --> 01:14:39,270
Right.

1257
01:14:39,810 --> 01:14:48,540
These phrases clearly articulate that the refusal is due to external mandates like laws or internal mandates like company policy.

1258
01:14:48,860 --> 01:14:50,220
It's not a personal preference.

1259
01:14:50,240 --> 01:14:53,430
It's about maintaining integrity and avoiding potential liabilities.

1260
01:14:53,730 --> 01:14:56,110
It's simply, look, this is the way we operate here.

1261
01:14:56,130 --> 01:14:56,850
Precisely.

1262
01:14:56,830 --> 01:14:57,990
Non-negotiable.

1263
01:14:58,110 --> 01:15:06,240
Okay, so let's look at some phrases for policy compliance and those ethical guidelines, starting with maybe a very clear objective policy violation.

1264
01:15:06,440 --> 01:15:06,840
How about this?

1265
01:15:07,160 --> 01:15:10,230
This hiring practice goes against company HR policy.

1266
01:15:10,350 --> 01:15:12,650
Direct, firm, can't argue with policy.

1267
01:15:12,750 --> 01:15:13,190
Pretty much.

1268
01:15:13,710 --> 01:15:17,740
It states that the proposed action directly contravenes established HR rules.

1269
01:15:18,380 --> 01:15:21,030
Leaves very little room for debate about personal discretion.

1270
01:15:21,570 --> 01:15:22,330
It's not your opinion.

1271
01:15:22,870 --> 01:15:24,680
It's company law, effectively.

1272
01:15:24,800 --> 01:15:25,820
No ambiguity there.

1273
01:15:26,040 --> 01:15:28,850
A clear professional boundary based on the rulebook.

1274
01:15:28,870 --> 01:15:31,190
Or for financial rules, something like...

1275
01:15:31,540 --> 01:15:34,990
I'm afraid that expense claim is not compliant with our travel guidelines.

1276
01:15:35,170 --> 01:15:37,150
Okay, compliant and guidelines.

1277
01:15:37,370 --> 01:15:37,950
Clear terms.

1278
01:15:38,010 --> 01:15:40,110
Yes, clear terms for rule adherence.

1279
01:15:40,860 --> 01:15:46,440
It explicitly states that the financial request doesn't meet the established internal standards.

1280
01:15:46,460 --> 01:15:51,090
It frames it as a procedural issue, not a judgment on the expense itself.

1281
01:15:51,110 --> 01:15:53,980
It's all about playing by the rules when it comes to money, right?

1282
01:15:54,150 --> 01:15:55,460
This one leaves no wiggle room.

1283
01:15:55,510 --> 01:15:56,410
Correct.

1284
01:15:56,430 --> 01:16:01,570
Now, when it comes to external regulations, mentioning the specific regulation can be very powerful.

1285
01:16:01,690 --> 01:16:02,640
Like GDPR.

1286
01:16:02,840 --> 01:16:03,380
Exactly.

1287
01:16:03,620 --> 01:16:09,950
For example, due to new GDPR regulatory requirements, we cannot proceed with this data request.

1288
01:16:09,970 --> 01:16:14,150
Using a specific example like GDPR immediately shows external legal constraints.

1289
01:16:14,880 --> 01:16:20,820
It shifts the reason for the decline from an internal choice to an unavoidable external legal obligation.

1290
01:16:20,850 --> 01:16:22,410
Which is pretty much non-negotiable.

1291
01:16:22,390 --> 01:16:22,930
Absolutely.

1292
01:16:22,990 --> 01:16:24,990
That immediately shuts down most arguments.

1293
01:16:25,010 --> 01:16:25,720
You're not saying no.

1294
01:16:25,740 --> 01:16:26,920
The law is saying no.

1295
01:16:26,940 --> 01:16:27,720
Makes sense.

1296
01:16:27,740 --> 01:16:30,610
And related to that, security and trust are obviously huge.

1297
01:16:31,030 --> 01:16:32,260
Paramount.

1298
01:16:32,270 --> 01:16:38,150
So you might say, this data sharing would breach our client data security protocols, so I must decline.

1299
01:16:38,300 --> 01:16:39,900
Reach and protocols.

1300
01:16:40,160 --> 01:16:40,790
Strong words.

1301
01:16:40,910 --> 01:16:41,230
They are.

1302
01:16:41,410 --> 01:16:43,950
They emphasize security and trust.

1303
01:16:43,970 --> 01:16:52,410
This phrase conveys that the request would compromise confidential information, which is a major risk and a professional red line you're obligated to uphold.

1304
01:16:53,070 --> 01:16:55,060
Protecting client data is fundamental.

1305
01:16:55,160 --> 01:17:02,590
It's a very strong protective no that signals you're prioritizing the security and trust placed in you by your clients.

1306
01:17:02,870 --> 01:17:04,670
No one can really argue with that principle.

1307
01:17:04,850 --> 01:17:05,880
It's hard to argue against.

1308
01:17:06,360 --> 01:17:11,930
And finally, a powerful phrase for broader ethical considerations beyond just legality or policy.

1309
01:17:12,470 --> 01:17:17,150
Our code of conduct has specific anti-bribery clauses that restrict this kind of activity.

1310
01:17:17,470 --> 01:17:19,060
Ah, referencing the code of conduct.

1311
01:17:19,040 --> 01:17:19,420
Yes.

1312
01:17:19,800 --> 01:17:24,530
This refers directly to ethical guidelines and core company values as the reason for refusal.

1313
01:17:24,870 --> 01:17:28,850
It positions the decline as upholding the organization's moral and ethical standing.

1314
01:17:28,890 --> 01:17:31,500
Which is fundamental to its reputation and integrity.

1315
01:17:31,940 --> 01:17:32,500
Exactly.

1316
01:17:32,580 --> 01:17:38,970
So it's not just about what's strictly legal, but what's considered right according to the company's stated values.

1317
01:17:39,310 --> 01:17:43,440
That's a no that reinforces the very fabric of the organization's culture.

1318
01:17:43,450 --> 01:17:44,300
Very important.

1319
01:17:44,990 --> 01:17:46,200
Okay, let's shift gears again.

1320
01:17:46,220 --> 01:17:49,200
Let's talk about scope creep and formal change requests.

1321
01:17:50,440 --> 01:17:51,800
Ah, scope creep.

1322
01:17:52,560 --> 01:17:55,230
The bane of every project manager's existence.

1323
01:17:55,660 --> 01:17:56,010
Chuckles.

1324
01:17:56,130 --> 01:17:56,750
Isn't it just?

1325
01:17:56,990 --> 01:17:59,470
It's that sneaky expansion of a project, isn't it?

1326
01:17:59,530 --> 01:18:01,110
Stuff that wasn't in the original plan.

1327
01:18:01,470 --> 01:18:03,010
And it can just derail everything.

1328
01:18:03,050 --> 01:18:04,770
Timelines, budgets, quality.

1329
01:18:04,790 --> 01:18:05,270
Completely.

1330
01:18:05,570 --> 01:18:06,190
It's insidious.

1331
01:18:06,300 --> 01:18:10,400
So how do we say no to that elegantly without seeming like we're just resisting new ideas?

1332
01:18:10,420 --> 01:18:11,520
Yeah, that's the balance.

1333
01:18:11,500 --> 01:18:18,510
This set of phrases is really crucial for maintaining project integrity and preventing that quiet drain on resources.

1334
01:18:18,850 --> 01:18:29,210
They work by explicitly naming the issue scope creep, often insisting on following a proper process and highlighting the potential negative impacts on timelines and budgets if we don't.

1335
01:18:29,450 --> 01:18:34,320
So it's about enforcing those agreed upon boundaries, which is essential for successful project delivery, right?

1336
01:18:34,300 --> 01:18:35,860
Absolutely essential.

1337
01:18:35,880 --> 01:18:38,260
So kicking it off, a simple flag.

1338
01:18:39,040 --> 01:18:42,790
This new feature seems beyond the original project scope.

1339
01:18:43,410 --> 01:18:43,650
Okay.

1340
01:18:44,230 --> 01:18:45,510
Beyond the original scope.

1341
01:18:45,650 --> 01:18:46,710
Clear indicator.

1342
01:18:46,730 --> 01:18:46,910
Right.

1343
01:18:47,170 --> 01:18:51,920
It signals that this new idea wasn't part of the initial plan, the blueprint we all agreed on.

1344
01:18:52,600 --> 01:18:55,920
And therefore, it needs separate consideration or maybe a formal change process.

1345
01:18:55,980 --> 01:18:58,120
It's a gentle flag just saying, hey, wait a minute.

1346
01:18:58,400 --> 01:18:59,670
This wasn't in the blueprint.

1347
01:18:59,690 --> 01:19:00,550
Simple, direct.

1348
01:19:00,850 --> 01:19:01,910
No beating around the bush.

1349
01:19:02,230 --> 01:19:11,310
And if you need to be clear about the impact of that scope creep, you could say, I'm concerned this new interface change might impact our development timeline and existing budget.

1350
01:19:11,760 --> 01:19:14,200
Ah, connecting it directly to time and money.

1351
01:19:14,240 --> 01:19:14,600
Yes.

1352
01:19:15,480 --> 01:19:19,450
This highlights the impact of the scope creep on those two critical project elements.

1353
01:19:19,470 --> 01:19:21,030
It signals that the new request isn't free.

1354
01:19:21,090 --> 01:19:25,760
It has clear resource implications that need to be properly addressed before just jumping in.

1355
01:19:25,940 --> 01:19:28,200
That's a great way to put the consequences right out there.

1356
01:19:28,340 --> 01:19:33,010
Makes it less about you resisting the idea and more about you protecting the project's overall health.

1357
01:19:33,470 --> 01:19:34,330
Exactly.

1358
01:19:34,420 --> 01:19:38,360
or you can explicitly name the issue and force a discussion.

1359
01:19:39,740 --> 01:19:44,640
This additional reporting request would constitute scope creep, which we should discuss first.

1360
01:19:44,890 --> 01:19:46,890
Actually using the term scope creep.

1361
01:19:47,050 --> 01:19:47,290
Yes.

1362
01:19:48,050 --> 01:19:49,490
This explicitly names it.

1363
01:19:50,090 --> 01:19:59,400
And scope creep is a pretty universally understood term in business for those incremental additions to workload that happen without a formal process.

1364
01:19:59,520 --> 01:19:59,820
Right.

1365
01:20:00,060 --> 01:20:06,630
And crucially, it insists on a discussion first before proceeding to manage expectations that agree on how to handle it.

1366
01:20:06,950 --> 01:20:08,410
Maybe it becomes phase two.

1367
01:20:08,480 --> 01:20:09,980
Maybe it needs more budget, et cetera.

1368
01:20:10,080 --> 01:20:10,300
Yeah.

1369
01:20:10,340 --> 01:20:12,160
Naming it directly can be powerful.

1370
01:20:12,140 --> 01:20:17,650
It flags the issue for anyone familiar with project management and immediately pushes for that proper conversation.

1371
01:20:17,770 --> 01:20:22,880
And when dealing with new features that maybe don't align with the core documentation that started the project?

1372
01:20:23,040 --> 01:20:25,420
Like the project charter or the statement of work.

1373
01:20:25,440 --> 01:20:25,920
Exactly.

1374
01:20:26,000 --> 01:20:31,350
You might say, let's review the original project charter and sell W before adding new marketing features like this.

1375
01:20:31,950 --> 01:20:34,320
Ah, appealing to the official documents.

1376
01:20:34,450 --> 01:20:34,690
Right.

1377
01:20:35,090 --> 01:20:42,900
This refers back to the official project documentation, the charter, the statement of work, SOW, as the agreed basis for the scope definition.

1378
01:20:43,630 --> 01:20:46,930
It grounds the discussion in established, signed-off agreements.

1379
01:20:47,110 --> 01:20:52,850
Makes it less about your personal resistance and more about adhering to the shared plan everyone agreed to initially.

1380
01:20:52,820 --> 01:20:53,410
Precisely.

1381
01:20:53,770 --> 01:20:56,310
It's like, remember that thing we all agreed to at the start?

1382
01:20:56,770 --> 01:20:58,610
Let's just check that first before we add more.

1383
01:20:58,880 --> 01:20:59,420
Very clever.

1384
01:20:59,740 --> 01:21:00,560
Very professional.

1385
01:21:00,720 --> 01:21:07,850
And finally, when a formal process is absolutely necessary, maybe with clients or stakeholders who keep adding things?

1386
01:21:08,030 --> 01:21:08,230
Right.

1387
01:21:08,510 --> 01:21:10,380
You need to set that boundary firmly.

1388
01:21:10,680 --> 01:21:15,960
We need a formal change request process if we're to add items beyond the agreed statement of work.

1389
01:21:15,940 --> 01:21:17,810
Insisting on the formal procedure.

1390
01:21:17,830 --> 01:21:18,070
Yes.

1391
01:21:18,650 --> 01:21:21,410
This insists on formal procedures for any scope changes.

1392
01:21:21,850 --> 01:21:27,950
It's particularly useful, as you say, when dealing with clients or stakeholders who frequently request additions ad hoc.

1393
01:21:27,970 --> 01:21:34,460
It sets a clear professional boundary and ensures that any changes are properly documented, assessed for impact, and officially approved.

1394
01:21:34,610 --> 01:21:37,070
That feels like the ultimate boundary setter, doesn't it?

1395
01:21:37,130 --> 01:21:40,980
It basically says, sure, we can potentially do this, but we're doing it by the book.

1396
01:21:41,200 --> 01:21:43,920
No more informal, off-the-cuff additions.

1397
01:21:44,220 --> 01:21:44,680
Exactly.

1398
01:21:44,720 --> 01:21:47,340
It brings structure and control back to the process.

1399
01:21:47,540 --> 01:21:47,900
Excellent.

1400
01:21:48,690 --> 01:21:51,390
Okay, one more category here before we get to the softer no's.

1401
01:21:51,890 --> 01:21:58,880
What about saying no simply because you don't have enough data or information to make an informed decision?

1402
01:21:58,860 --> 01:21:59,960
Ah, yes.

1403
01:22:00,100 --> 01:22:03,420
The I can't say yes because I don't know enough situation.

1404
01:22:03,500 --> 01:22:03,780
Right.

1405
01:22:03,960 --> 01:22:07,290
This isn't really a firm refusal of the request itself, is it?

1406
01:22:07,350 --> 01:22:08,410
It's more like a pause.

1407
01:22:08,910 --> 01:22:12,370
It indicates a crucial barrier to being able to say yes responsibly.

1408
01:22:12,600 --> 01:22:13,020
Exactly.

1409
01:22:13,230 --> 01:22:18,120
It's about protecting yourself and the organization from making decisions effectively in the dark.

1410
01:22:19,020 --> 01:22:25,370
This category of no is really about asserting the need for sufficient information to ensure quality decision making.

1411
01:22:25,500 --> 01:22:31,950
So you're not saying no to the request necessarily, but you're saying no to proceeding without proper justification or context.

1412
01:22:32,370 --> 01:22:32,850
Precisely.

1413
01:22:33,550 --> 01:22:36,350
It protects against speculative or uninformed actions.

1414
01:22:36,950 --> 01:22:44,540
It ensures that your contributions, your decisions, your actions are always grounded in fact and evidence as much as possible.

1415
01:22:44,620 --> 01:22:45,080
Makes sense.

1416
01:22:45,640 --> 01:22:48,310
So let's start with the basic but powerful one here.

1417
01:22:48,590 --> 01:22:52,810
How about, I like the sales data needed for this Q3 forecast.

1418
01:22:52,960 --> 01:22:53,720
Very specific.

1419
01:22:53,840 --> 01:22:55,220
Identifies the missing piece.

1420
01:22:55,740 --> 01:22:55,940
Right.

1421
01:22:56,650 --> 01:23:07,070
It clearly states that a crucial decision, like a forecast, relies on specific data that you currently don't have, making it impossible, or at least irresponsible, to proceed.

1422
01:23:07,410 --> 01:23:10,710
It's very direct, highlighting the absence of that critical component.

1423
01:23:10,780 --> 01:23:12,120
No data, no decision.

1424
01:23:12,480 --> 01:23:13,000
Simple as that.

1425
01:23:13,060 --> 01:23:14,300
It's a logical wall they've hit.

1426
01:23:14,600 --> 01:23:15,020
Pretty much.

1427
01:23:15,500 --> 01:23:20,070
For broader strategic moves, maybe launching a new product, you might need more than just one data point.

1428
01:23:20,390 --> 01:23:23,850
More comprehensive market data is needed before a new product decision can be made.

1429
01:23:24,210 --> 01:23:26,770
Okay, emphasizing the need for thorough analysis.

1430
01:23:26,790 --> 01:23:26,990
Right.

1431
01:23:27,430 --> 01:23:31,560
Before making significant, potentially expensive decisions like a product launch.

1432
01:23:31,580 --> 01:23:37,980
It protects against launching products into an unknown or inadequately researched market, which is unfortunately a common pitfall.

1433
01:23:38,170 --> 01:23:42,200
It's essentially saying, let's not just throw spaghetti at the wall and hope it sticks.

1434
01:23:42,300 --> 01:23:45,580
Let's make sure we're actually aiming for something real.

1435
01:23:45,830 --> 01:23:46,530
Good analogy.

1436
01:23:47,490 --> 01:23:52,480
And when dealing with financial commitments, you have to be extra careful, obviously.

1437
01:23:52,580 --> 01:23:52,800
Yeah.

1438
01:23:53,000 --> 01:23:58,960
So you might say, I cannot act on this investment without further financial details or supporting evidence.

1439
01:23:59,160 --> 01:24:01,610
Requires more specifics before committing money.

1440
01:24:01,910 --> 01:24:02,390
Exactly.

1441
01:24:03,030 --> 01:24:12,060
It's a necessary safeguard against making ill-informed investment decisions based on insufficient or maybe unverified information.

1442
01:24:12,620 --> 01:24:14,600
Due diligence is absolutely key here.

1443
01:24:14,680 --> 01:24:17,230
So you're not saying no to the investment opportunity itself.

1444
01:24:17,250 --> 01:24:19,510
You're just saying no to a blind investment.

1445
01:24:19,950 --> 01:24:20,910
Very responsible.

1446
01:24:21,150 --> 01:24:21,480
Very.

1447
01:24:21,910 --> 01:24:26,080
And sometimes the information you have received is just plain inadequate, you could say.

1448
01:24:26,520 --> 01:24:30,450
The current customer information provided is inadequate for me to take this campaign forward.

1449
01:24:30,630 --> 01:24:33,010
States clearly the data isn't good enough.

1450
01:24:32,990 --> 01:24:33,270
Right.

1451
01:24:33,430 --> 01:24:37,120
It states the supply data is insufficient for you to take effective action.

1452
01:24:37,920 --> 01:24:48,440
This is crucial for things like marketing or sales campaigns that require specific, robust customer profiles for effective targeting and ultimately a successful outreach.

1453
01:24:49,020 --> 01:24:50,080
Garbage in, garbage out.

1454
01:24:50,420 --> 01:24:50,540
Right.

1455
01:24:50,560 --> 01:24:51,120
Exactly.

1456
01:24:51,520 --> 01:24:53,240
So it's not that you can't do the campaign.

1457
01:24:53,320 --> 01:24:56,390
It's that you can't do it well with the poor information you've been given.

1458
01:24:56,470 --> 01:24:59,930
It protects the quality of your work and avoids wasting resources.

1459
01:24:59,990 --> 01:25:00,670
Precisely.

1460
01:25:00,650 --> 01:25:04,080
And finally, one that cleverly shifts some responsibility for getting the data.

1461
01:25:05,220 --> 01:25:10,730
Is there a way we can obtain the necessary historical sales data to support this market request adequately?

1462
01:25:11,570 --> 01:25:13,590
Ah, turning it into a collaborative question.

1463
01:25:13,650 --> 01:25:13,890
Yes.

1464
01:25:14,510 --> 01:25:17,520
It offers a collaborative approach to acquiring that missing data.

1465
01:25:18,040 --> 01:25:21,400
It shows you're willing to work together to overcome the information barrier.

1466
01:25:22,140 --> 01:25:26,350
Especially useful when historical trends are crucial for making a solid market decision.

1467
01:25:26,970 --> 01:25:29,710
It turns a potential roadblock into a shared task.

1468
01:25:29,810 --> 01:25:36,500
That's a good one because it's not just a no, I don't have it, but more like, OK, how can we get what we need to potentially make this a yes?

1469
01:25:37,120 --> 01:25:39,610
It keeps that problem solving mindset alive.

1470
01:25:40,110 --> 01:25:40,570
Exactly.

1471
01:25:40,610 --> 01:25:42,190
It keeps the door open for progress.

1472
01:25:42,810 --> 01:25:43,730
OK, this is great.

1473
01:25:43,760 --> 01:25:49,590
We've covered the no's based on capacity, expertise, resources, policy, lack of data.

1474
01:25:51,030 --> 01:25:53,270
But not all no's are hard stops, are they?

1475
01:25:53,340 --> 01:25:54,260
No, not at all.

1476
01:25:54,320 --> 01:25:56,320
This raises that important question again.

1477
01:25:57,060 --> 01:26:00,600
How can we say no in a way that's actually helpful and constructive?

1478
01:26:00,760 --> 01:26:01,000
Right.

1479
01:26:01,340 --> 01:26:03,390
Not all no's have to be outright rejections.

1480
01:26:03,750 --> 01:26:04,810
Many are more nuanced.

1481
01:26:05,150 --> 01:26:10,850
They're about redirection, maybe negotiation, or sometimes just buying yourself time to make a properly informed decision.

1482
01:26:10,970 --> 01:26:17,200
So this is where active problem solving comes in, turning a potential rejection into more of a collaborative step forward.

1483
01:26:17,180 --> 01:26:17,880
Exactly.

1484
01:26:18,040 --> 01:26:25,090
It helps maintain goodwill, and often it actually leads to better outcomes for everyone involved because you're refining their request or the approach.

1485
01:26:25,370 --> 01:26:27,730
It's about opening a dialogue, not just closing a door.

1486
01:26:27,770 --> 01:26:34,640
These soft no's, they demonstrate a willingness to engage, but maybe with necessary conditions or clarifications first.

1487
01:26:34,740 --> 01:26:34,940
Right.

1488
01:26:35,440 --> 01:26:44,760
It shows you're not just dismissing a request out of hand, but you're actively trying to find the best path forward, which frankly often makes you look even more capable and considerate.

1489
01:26:44,850 --> 01:26:51,090
Okay, so let's explore some phrases for just requesting more information or clarification before deciding.

1490
01:26:51,620 --> 01:26:55,790
This effectively buys you time, right, to assess properly.

1491
01:26:56,030 --> 01:26:59,990
Yes, it buys time and ensures clarity, starting with a very simple one.

1492
01:27:00,010 --> 01:27:01,690
I need more details on the client first.

1493
01:27:01,930 --> 01:27:03,330
Basic, direct.

1494
01:27:03,550 --> 01:27:06,540
It is a simple, direct request for context.

1495
01:27:07,180 --> 01:27:15,160
It signals that fundamental background information is missing, which is essential for you to properly assess the request and respond appropriately.

1496
01:27:15,610 --> 01:27:18,070
It's just a fundamental step in good communications.

1497
01:27:18,250 --> 01:27:23,180
So not a refusal, just a, hold on, I need more info before I can give you a proper answer.

1498
01:27:23,500 --> 01:27:23,800
Smart.

1499
01:27:23,980 --> 01:27:26,280
Or for something a bit more complex, maybe a project.

1500
01:27:26,460 --> 01:27:26,800
Yeah.

1501
01:27:26,820 --> 01:27:29,410
I'll need a bit more context on the project before I can commit.

1502
01:27:29,510 --> 01:27:29,910
Okay.

1503
01:27:29,930 --> 01:27:30,910
Needing the bigger picture.

1504
01:27:31,270 --> 01:27:31,490
Right.

1505
01:27:31,590 --> 01:27:36,580
This emphasizes needing a broader understanding of the project's background, its goals, maybe its current status.

1506
01:27:36,940 --> 01:27:44,630
It's really useful when you're asked to join a new project and you need that bigger picture to contribute effectively and importantly, avoid making missteps early on.

1507
01:27:44,930 --> 01:27:48,950
Yeah, you're protecting yourself from diving into something without fully grasping it.

1508
01:27:49,390 --> 01:27:53,150
Which is actually a proactive way to avoid future problems down the line.

1509
01:27:53,330 --> 01:27:53,730
Definitely.

1510
01:27:54,230 --> 01:27:56,810
And for ensuring strategic alignment, which we talked about.

1511
01:27:57,590 --> 01:28:02,360
Before I can give a definitive answer, I need to understand the project's objectives.

1512
01:28:02,380 --> 01:28:03,480
Focusing on the why.

1513
01:28:03,680 --> 01:28:04,300
Exactly.

1514
01:28:04,680 --> 01:28:10,810
This emphasizes understanding the why behind the task before committing your time or resources.

1515
01:28:11,730 --> 01:28:16,790
Ensuring you're aligned with the core goals is fundamental to making an effective contribution.

1516
01:28:17,310 --> 01:28:19,960
Otherwise, you could end up working hard on the wrong things.

1517
01:28:20,240 --> 01:28:25,370
Yeah, it's the difference between just doing tasks and doing meaningful work that contributes to the goal.

1518
01:28:25,790 --> 01:28:27,970
You're trying to make sure your effort is well-directed.

1519
01:28:28,270 --> 01:28:28,490
Right.

1520
01:28:28,690 --> 01:28:32,220
Yeah, sometimes getting that clarity requires more than just an email.

1521
01:28:32,400 --> 01:28:32,920
A meeting.

1522
01:28:33,200 --> 01:28:33,520
Could be.

1523
01:28:33,820 --> 01:28:34,900
You could suggest.

1524
01:28:35,320 --> 01:28:39,290
Perhaps we could schedule a brief meeting to discuss the technical requirements in detail.

1525
01:28:39,530 --> 01:28:40,980
Okay, proposing a conversation.

1526
01:28:41,160 --> 01:28:48,370
Yes, it suggests a face-to-face or virtual face-to-face discussion to iron out complexities.

1527
01:28:49,330 --> 01:28:58,850
Especially for complex technical details that are just hard to convey accurately in writing, a short conversation can be far more efficient and prevent costly misunderstandings.

1528
01:28:58,930 --> 01:29:02,910
It's like saying, look, let's not email back and forth for three days about this.

1529
01:29:03,050 --> 01:29:05,320
A quick 15-minute chat will probably sort it.

1530
01:29:05,740 --> 01:29:06,460
Very practical.

1531
01:29:06,580 --> 01:29:06,860
Very.

1532
01:29:07,480 --> 01:29:16,630
And finally, for managing your own workload when new requests come in, it would be helpful to know the priority of this task relative to other current tasks.

1533
01:29:17,190 --> 01:29:18,450
Ah, asking about priority.

1534
01:29:18,570 --> 01:29:18,830
Yes.

1535
01:29:19,480 --> 01:29:23,500
This asks for clarification on its urgency compared to your other existing work.

1536
01:29:24,050 --> 01:29:33,090
For managing multiple requests effectively, understanding the comparative priority is absolutely key to good time management and to ensuring you're working on the most impactful items first.

1537
01:29:33,470 --> 01:29:38,760
And that's also you gently pushing the prioritization question back to the requester or maybe to your manager, isn't it?

1538
01:29:39,160 --> 01:29:42,810
Asking them to help you decide what might need to be dropped if this new thing comes in.

1539
01:29:42,850 --> 01:29:44,010
That's good boundary setting.

1540
01:29:44,170 --> 01:29:44,490
It is.

1541
01:29:44,590 --> 01:29:46,970
It forces a conscious decision about priorities.

1542
01:29:47,070 --> 01:29:51,380
Okay, so requesting more info is one type of soft no.

1543
01:29:52,260 --> 01:29:56,170
What about actively suggesting alternatives or compromises?

1544
01:29:57,370 --> 01:30:02,240
This is where you say no to the specific request, but maybe yes to finding a solution.

1545
01:30:02,390 --> 01:30:02,910
Precisely.

1546
01:30:03,640 --> 01:30:09,320
These phrases allow you to pivot gracefully from a direct refusal into a collaborative problem-solving mode.

1547
01:30:09,900 --> 01:30:15,650
It shows you're not just dismissing their need or their request, but you're actively trying to find a viable path forward.

1548
01:30:16,250 --> 01:30:19,180
Demonstrates flexibility, helpful attitude.

1549
01:30:19,280 --> 01:30:23,080
So instead of just a flat no, you might offer a different approach.

1550
01:30:23,100 --> 01:30:25,780
Like, how about trying a cloud-based solution instead?

1551
01:30:25,770 --> 01:30:26,390
Exactly.

1552
01:30:26,590 --> 01:30:28,770
That offers a specific alternative approach.

1553
01:30:28,910 --> 01:30:31,790
It's useful when the solution they proposed is problematic.

1554
01:30:32,150 --> 01:30:38,120
For some reason, maybe it's too expensive, too complex, not secure enough, and you have a better, more feasible option in mind.

1555
01:30:38,140 --> 01:30:41,400
You're not just rejecting, you're innovating, offering a different path.

1556
01:30:41,640 --> 01:30:44,670
Or if it's about who should do it, not what should be done.

1557
01:30:44,790 --> 01:30:47,030
Perhaps Sarah Miller from marketing could assist with this.

1558
01:30:47,300 --> 01:30:49,920
Yes, suggesting a specific colleague.

1559
01:30:50,600 --> 01:30:57,850
It's effective when you genuinely know someone else is a better fit, maybe has the right skills, or, importantly, more capacity.

1560
01:30:58,550 --> 01:31:06,180
It leverages the organization's resources effectively and ensures the task still gets done by the right person, even if it's not you.

1561
01:31:06,160 --> 01:31:12,130
That feels like a real value add, connecting people to the right resources, even when you're saying no yourself.

1562
01:31:12,250 --> 01:31:12,530
It is.

1563
01:31:13,010 --> 01:31:18,640
Now, for larger commitments, like maybe an all-day workshop or event, you can offer partial participation.

1564
01:31:19,240 --> 01:31:23,120
While I can't attend the full day, I could possibly join the morning session.

1565
01:31:23,220 --> 01:31:24,660
Okay, offering a compromise.

1566
01:31:24,690 --> 01:31:25,170
Right.

1567
01:31:25,190 --> 01:31:26,670
It offers partial participation.

1568
01:31:27,030 --> 01:31:35,060
It's perfect for those events or long meetings where full attendance isn't possible for you, but you still want to contribute or at least be present for the key segments.

1569
01:31:35,090 --> 01:31:41,360
It's like saying, look, I can't give you 100 percent of my day, but I can give you the most impactful 30 percent or the part that's most relevant.

1570
01:31:41,480 --> 01:31:42,980
Very strategic use of time.

1571
01:31:43,100 --> 01:31:43,440
Very.

1572
01:31:43,980 --> 01:31:49,270
And if the core issue preventing a yes is the deadline, you can try negotiating that.

1573
01:31:49,810 --> 01:31:53,970
If the report deadline is flexible, I might be able to tackle it later on next week.

1574
01:31:53,950 --> 01:31:59,330
Proposing a revised timeline, but making it conditional on their flexibility.

1575
01:31:59,590 --> 01:32:00,090
Exactly.

1576
01:32:00,710 --> 01:32:03,820
It's excellent when the deadline is the main sticking point.

1577
01:32:03,840 --> 01:32:09,090
And you know that a slight adjustment could realistically turn your no into a yes.

1578
01:32:09,120 --> 01:32:11,290
That's a key negotiation point, isn't it?

1579
01:32:11,370 --> 01:32:17,070
You're implicitly asking how truly important is this specific deadline versus the actual completion of the task.

1580
01:32:17,270 --> 01:32:17,760
Precisely.

1581
01:32:18,360 --> 01:32:20,240
Forces them to think about what matters more.

1582
01:32:20,880 --> 01:32:25,100
And finally, for those really big, daunting projects, you could suggest breaking it down.

1583
01:32:25,780 --> 01:32:30,070
If you're open to suggestions, perhaps phasing the software project could work for the team.

1584
01:32:30,050 --> 01:32:34,540
Ah, suggesting a phased approach, like eating the elephant one bite at a time?

1585
01:32:34,700 --> 01:32:35,040
Chuckles.

1586
01:32:35,300 --> 01:32:36,100
Exactly like that.

1587
01:32:36,580 --> 01:32:38,800
It suggests a phased approach for large projects.

1588
01:32:39,260 --> 01:32:43,150
It breaks down what might feel like an overwhelming request into more manageable chunks.

1589
01:32:43,650 --> 01:32:50,660
This makes it more feasible for the team to take on over time and often improves the likelihood of success because you can learn and adapt between phases.

1590
01:32:50,910 --> 01:32:58,460
So it's not, no, we can't possibly do this massive thing, but more like, no, we can't do it all at once, but maybe we can do it piece by piece.

1591
01:32:58,660 --> 01:33:00,540
That's just smart project management, really.

1592
01:33:00,760 --> 01:33:01,680
It absolutely is.

1593
01:33:01,700 --> 01:33:03,000
Okay, we've covered a lot of ground.

1594
01:33:03,420 --> 01:33:14,420
But sometimes, sometimes, after all the considerations, all the alternatives, all the clarifications, you just have to give a direct, unequivocal no.

1595
01:33:14,800 --> 01:33:15,200
Right.

1596
01:33:15,340 --> 01:33:15,560
Yes.

1597
01:33:16,180 --> 01:33:18,090
Sometimes that's the only appropriate answer.

1598
01:33:18,110 --> 01:33:20,190
You just have to draw that clear line in the sand.

1599
01:33:20,350 --> 01:33:23,290
But the key is you can still do it with grace and respect.

1600
01:33:23,850 --> 01:33:29,360
So this final section is about maintaining politeness while being absolutely clear and firm.

1601
01:33:29,560 --> 01:33:30,120
Precisely.

1602
01:33:30,400 --> 01:33:34,950
This is about maintaining your boundaries firmly, but without causing unnecessary offense.

1603
01:33:35,270 --> 01:33:41,330
These phrases are for those situations when a clear, unequivocal refusal is the only appropriate response.

1604
01:33:41,310 --> 01:33:44,520
Ensuring you protect your time, your focus, maybe your values.

1605
01:33:44,580 --> 01:33:44,780
Right.

1606
01:33:45,200 --> 01:33:48,400
While still respecting the relationship and your professional standing.

1607
01:33:48,920 --> 01:33:52,190
It's about projecting confidence in your decision, not apology for it.

1608
01:33:52,470 --> 01:33:53,150
Okay.

1609
01:33:53,170 --> 01:34:00,520
So let's dive into phrases for just general polite declines, maybe for tasks or projects, starting with something straightforward.

1610
01:34:00,940 --> 01:34:04,870
How about, I must decline taking on this project this time.

1611
01:34:05,290 --> 01:34:05,390
Okay.

1612
01:34:05,990 --> 01:34:07,170
Must decline.

1613
01:34:07,830 --> 01:34:08,430
Sounds firm.

1614
01:34:08,450 --> 01:34:08,550
Okay.

1615
01:34:08,670 --> 01:34:09,030
It is.

1616
01:34:09,110 --> 01:34:11,350
It's a straightforward, definitive refusal.

1617
01:34:12,490 --> 01:34:14,860
But notice the addition of this time.

1618
01:34:15,660 --> 01:34:17,180
Yeah, that softens it slightly.

1619
01:34:17,520 --> 01:34:19,570
It subtly leaves a small opening for the future.

1620
01:34:20,190 --> 01:34:24,920
It suggests the circumstances might change down the road, but for right now, the answer is clearly no.

1621
01:34:25,600 --> 01:34:28,480
It maintains politeness while being unequivocally clear.

1622
01:34:28,530 --> 01:34:31,250
So it's firm, but not necessarily a permanent door slam.

1623
01:34:31,710 --> 01:34:31,810
Okay.

1624
01:34:32,430 --> 01:34:35,020
How about something a bit gentler, but still firm?

1625
01:34:35,160 --> 01:34:39,240
You could use, I'm sorry, but that particular request won't be possible for me to handle.

1626
01:34:39,400 --> 01:34:40,980
Using I'm sorry to cushion it.

1627
01:34:41,160 --> 01:34:48,070
Yes, I'm sorry softens the blow, but it's immediately followed by a clear statement of impossibility won't be possible for me to handle.

1628
01:34:48,470 --> 01:34:54,000
It's a direct but gentle refusal, emphasizing that it's simply not within your current capacity or ability to do it.

1629
01:34:54,020 --> 01:34:55,660
Yeah, that's a classic for a reason.

1630
01:34:56,040 --> 01:34:58,280
Empathetic opening, but a clear no.

1631
01:34:58,410 --> 01:35:02,380
Now, if you want to convey that you've really thought about it before saying no.

1632
01:35:02,600 --> 01:35:03,870
Yeah, that can be important.

1633
01:35:03,990 --> 01:35:10,220
You could say, after careful consideration of my schedule, I've determined I cannot take this new task on.

1634
01:35:10,580 --> 01:35:12,800
Highlighting the careful consideration.

1635
01:35:12,940 --> 01:35:13,600
Exactly.

1636
01:35:14,040 --> 01:35:16,730
It highlights thoughtful deliberation before the refusal.

1637
01:35:17,310 --> 01:35:24,300
It conveys that the decision wasn't just a knee-jerk reaction, but a considered choice based on reviewing your commitments.

1638
01:35:24,940 --> 01:35:27,080
That lends it more weight and professionalism.

1639
01:35:27,120 --> 01:35:29,740
Makes it harder for someone to argue with your decision, perhaps.

1640
01:35:30,140 --> 01:35:33,890
That one screams, look, I really did think about this, and this is my firm conclusion.

1641
01:35:34,150 --> 01:35:35,070
Right.

1642
01:35:35,050 --> 01:35:43,000
For declining significant opportunities, maybe a leadership role or a prestigious project, it's really important to express gratitude first.

1643
01:35:43,140 --> 01:35:43,480
Okay.

1644
01:35:43,820 --> 01:35:49,990
So something like, thank you for the leadership opportunity, but I must respectfully turn it down at this moment.

1645
01:35:50,010 --> 01:35:52,560
Expresses gratitude, then politely declines.

1646
01:35:53,030 --> 01:35:53,280
Yes.

1647
01:35:54,060 --> 01:35:58,540
It's important to acknowledge the honor or significance of the offer, even when you cannot accept it.

1648
01:35:59,550 --> 01:36:04,010
This helps preserve goodwill and shows respect for the person offering it.

1649
01:36:04,210 --> 01:36:10,860
The at this moment also adds that touch of potential future possibility, perhaps.

1650
01:36:10,940 --> 01:36:16,550
Yeah, it acknowledges the value of the offer while being very clear about your current inability to take it on.

1651
01:36:16,810 --> 01:36:17,450
Good balance.

1652
01:36:17,490 --> 01:36:23,260
And sometimes the reason for declining is genuinely personal, but you can still phrase it professionally.

1653
01:36:23,320 --> 01:36:23,480
Right.

1654
01:36:23,520 --> 01:36:26,480
You don't want to overshare, but sometimes context helps.

1655
01:36:26,510 --> 01:36:26,930
Exactly.

1656
01:36:26,950 --> 01:36:28,110
You can say...

1657
01:36:28,090 --> 01:36:32,150
I wish I could assist with the conference, but I must decline due to other pressing family matters.

1658
01:36:32,340 --> 01:36:32,480
Okay.

1659
01:36:32,500 --> 01:36:34,380
It gives a reason, but keeps it general.

1660
01:36:34,600 --> 01:36:34,800
Right.

1661
01:36:34,860 --> 01:36:38,750
It gives a personal, but still professional sounding reason for declining.

1662
01:36:39,570 --> 01:36:48,540
It provides context without going into unnecessary detail, which is often appreciated as it shows you're a human being with a life outside of work, but maintains boundaries.

1663
01:36:48,860 --> 01:36:52,330
It's honest, but still keeps that professional boundary firmly intact.

1664
01:36:52,790 --> 01:36:55,110
Okay, that covers general task project declines.

1665
01:36:55,750 --> 01:36:58,530
What about the everyday stuff, declining invitations and meetings?

1666
01:36:58,560 --> 01:36:59,580
We all get loads of those.

1667
01:37:00,080 --> 01:37:00,780
Oh, absolutely.

1668
01:37:01,380 --> 01:37:03,540
These are daily occurrences for many of us, right?

1669
01:37:03,800 --> 01:37:08,750
So you need quick, polite, almost reflexive ways to say no to those.

1670
01:37:09,050 --> 01:37:09,790
Yeah.

1671
01:37:09,810 --> 01:37:15,900
These phrases focus on brevity and clarity, especially for social invites or less formal business meetings.

1672
01:37:15,880 --> 01:37:21,260
They're about politely stating your unavailability without needing a long, drawn out explanation.

1673
01:37:21,420 --> 01:37:24,050
And often they leave room for future engagement.

1674
01:37:24,470 --> 01:37:28,710
The key is being respectful of the invitation while being firm about your schedule.

1675
01:37:28,890 --> 01:37:29,450
Exactly.

1676
01:37:29,730 --> 01:37:32,300
So for quick declines, you can just go with the basics.

1677
01:37:32,310 --> 01:37:35,840
Sorry, I can't make it to the dinner or perhaps I won't be able to attend the conference call.

1678
01:37:36,040 --> 01:37:39,000
Simple, direct, gets the job done without over explaining.

1679
01:37:39,200 --> 01:37:40,060
Deficient.

1680
01:37:40,750 --> 01:37:41,110
vary.

1681
01:37:41,510 --> 01:37:44,820
For maybe a team social event, adding appreciation is always nice.

1682
01:37:45,560 --> 01:37:48,100
Thank you for the team lunch invitation, but I'm unavailable.

1683
01:37:48,480 --> 01:37:48,780
Simple.

1684
01:37:49,390 --> 01:37:57,440
And if you have a clear specific conflict, a straightforward, I have a prior client commitment at that time, unfortunately, is universally understood in business.

1685
01:37:58,340 --> 01:37:59,300
Client comes first.

1686
01:37:59,440 --> 01:37:59,660
Right.

1687
01:37:59,680 --> 01:38:01,280
That's a reason most people won't argue with.

1688
01:38:01,880 --> 01:38:04,350
What about more formal meetings or important sessions?

1689
01:38:04,330 --> 01:38:05,650
You might step it up slightly.

1690
01:38:06,510 --> 01:38:11,160
Regrettably, I will have to miss this strategy session due to a schedule conflict.

1691
01:38:11,820 --> 01:38:13,700
Regrettably adds a touch of formality.

1692
01:38:14,200 --> 01:38:14,520
Okay.

1693
01:38:14,980 --> 01:38:16,440
Or for something like a webinar invite.

1694
01:38:17,170 --> 01:38:20,870
I appreciate the invite to the webinar, but my schedule is already packed on that day.

1695
01:38:21,910 --> 01:38:22,070
Blight.

1696
01:38:22,370 --> 01:38:22,590
Okay.

1697
01:38:22,610 --> 01:38:24,640
Explains the busyness without needing specifics.

1698
01:38:24,960 --> 01:38:28,660
And if you do want to leave the door open for rescheduling a meeting...

1699
01:38:28,690 --> 01:38:29,840
Ah, good point.

1700
01:38:30,640 --> 01:38:31,160
You could try.

1701
01:38:31,200 --> 01:38:33,970
Unfortunately, that timing for the meeting doesn't work for me.

1702
01:38:34,020 --> 01:38:34,850
Perhaps another time.

1703
01:38:35,230 --> 01:38:38,900
Direct about the timing issue, but explicitly asks about alternatives.

1704
01:38:39,020 --> 01:38:39,230
Right.

1705
01:38:39,580 --> 01:38:46,130
It opens the door for rescheduling, showing you're willing to engage if the conditions, namely the time, change.

1706
01:38:46,360 --> 01:38:46,820
Okay.

1707
01:38:47,240 --> 01:38:50,170
And for bigger asks, like maybe a speaking invitation you mentioned?

1708
01:38:50,230 --> 01:38:50,450
Yes.

1709
01:38:50,550 --> 01:38:52,070
You'd want to be respectful of the honor.

1710
01:38:52,550 --> 01:38:56,950
I'm honored by the speaking invitation, but I must decline due to existing obligations.

1711
01:38:57,630 --> 01:39:01,660
You're showing respect for the offer while stating non-negotiable prior commitments.

1712
01:39:01,800 --> 01:39:02,160
Good one.

1713
01:39:02,480 --> 01:39:05,640
And is there a classic way to decline but invite future contact?

1714
01:39:06,060 --> 01:39:07,080
Yes, definitely.

1715
01:39:07,140 --> 01:39:09,870
Something like, I received your meeting invitation.

1716
01:39:10,530 --> 01:39:11,950
Sadly, I won't be able to make it.

1717
01:39:12,410 --> 01:39:13,770
Keep me in mind for the next one.

1718
01:39:13,950 --> 01:39:18,590
Acknowledges receipt, expresses regret, asks to be kept in the loop.

1719
01:39:18,870 --> 01:39:19,390
Exactly.

1720
01:39:19,650 --> 01:39:27,780
It acknowledges you got it, expresses polite regret, and offers a clear path for future invitations, maintaining that valuable professional connection.

1721
01:39:27,860 --> 01:39:34,670
Yeah, it's all about being unavailable for this specific thing without being ungracious or seeming uninterested overall.

1722
01:39:34,710 --> 01:39:35,370
Very effective.

1723
01:39:35,450 --> 01:39:36,030
Precisely.

1724
01:39:36,290 --> 01:39:44,240
Okay, lastly then, let's talk about those phrases for drawing a really firm line and protecting your boundaries based specifically on your priorities.

1725
01:39:45,080 --> 01:39:46,540
Ah, yes, this is crucial.

1726
01:39:46,580 --> 01:39:51,690
This is crucial for maintaining focus and accountability to your core responsibilities, isn't it?

1727
01:39:51,670 --> 01:39:59,000
It's not about not wanting to do something necessarily, but about recognizing that other things already on your list are simply more important right now.

1728
01:39:59,280 --> 01:39:59,820
Absolutely.

1729
01:40:00,260 --> 01:40:07,000
These phrases are all about clearly communicating your strategic priorities, making it explicit that your no isn't just arbitrary.

1730
01:40:07,560 --> 01:40:11,570
It's based on a predefined strategy or a pre-agreed list of tasks.

1731
01:40:12,070 --> 01:40:14,330
They demonstrate that you are organized.

1732
01:40:14,550 --> 01:40:17,030
You're accountable for your existing commitments.

1733
01:40:17,020 --> 01:40:24,600
And they often allow you to gracefully shift the burden of prioritization back to the requester or maybe to the organizational system itself.

1734
01:40:24,780 --> 01:40:25,060
Okay.

1735
01:40:25,320 --> 01:40:29,110
So for a quick and clear priority statement, maybe for a lower level task.

1736
01:40:29,290 --> 01:40:32,870
You could just say, this data entry isn't top priority right now.

1737
01:40:33,130 --> 01:40:33,570
Direct.

1738
01:40:34,200 --> 01:40:35,540
States it's low priority.

1739
01:40:35,860 --> 01:40:36,060
Right.

1740
01:40:36,180 --> 01:40:37,960
States it's low priority without judgment.

1741
01:40:38,260 --> 01:40:44,570
It's simple, immediate, and effective in managing expectations about when or maybe even if it will get done soon.

1742
01:40:44,910 --> 01:40:46,970
Or for something more critical that's blocking it.

1743
01:40:47,000 --> 01:40:50,930
You might say, I must focus on other urgent client items first.

1744
01:40:51,610 --> 01:40:54,920
This highlights a non-negotiable, higher priority, the client.

1745
01:40:55,380 --> 01:41:01,130
It clearly communicates that client-facing work takes precedence, which is a universally understood business imperative.

1746
01:41:01,270 --> 01:41:02,990
Can't argue with focusing on the client.

1747
01:41:03,510 --> 01:41:05,740
And a broader statement about your current focus.

1748
01:41:05,850 --> 01:41:06,490
you could use.

1749
01:41:07,400 --> 01:41:10,040
My current work priorities lie elsewhere at the moment.

1750
01:41:11,130 --> 01:41:15,880
This broadly indicates that your focus is simply on other more pressing tasks.

1751
01:41:16,680 --> 01:41:20,230
Tasks that have already been established as your key responsibilities for this period.

1752
01:41:20,300 --> 01:41:25,970
It's a polite way to say your plate is full, but also that the items already on it are ranked higher.

1753
01:41:26,470 --> 01:41:30,750
My energy and attention are already allocated to what's been identified as most important.

1754
01:41:30,990 --> 01:41:31,450
Exactly.

1755
01:41:31,650 --> 01:41:37,620
Now, when a new task maybe conflicts with your formally agreed upon goals, like in project management.

1756
01:41:37,650 --> 01:41:38,670
Right, like sprint goals.

1757
01:41:38,850 --> 01:41:39,370
Precisely.

1758
01:41:39,610 --> 01:41:45,580
You could say, this new feature task, while important, doesn't align with my immediate sprint objectives.

1759
01:41:45,910 --> 01:41:50,780
This uses that agile terminology to explain the misalignment with current time-bound goals.

1760
01:41:51,900 --> 01:41:59,410
It's very specific to certain project methodologies, but it signals a planned workflow that shouldn't ideally be disrupted mid-sprint.

1761
01:41:59,430 --> 01:42:04,800
Yeah, this one makes it clear that you're adhering to a predefined, probably team-based work plan.

1762
01:42:04,880 --> 01:42:06,800
It's not just your personal choice holding it back.

1763
01:42:06,960 --> 01:42:07,260
Correct.

1764
01:42:07,440 --> 01:42:10,280
And if you need help from the requester in actually figuring out the priority.

1765
01:42:10,790 --> 01:42:11,850
Ah, putting it back on them.

1766
01:42:12,230 --> 01:42:12,450
Yes.

1767
01:42:13,290 --> 01:42:14,450
You could ask.

1768
01:42:14,600 --> 01:42:21,070
Before I can slot this research in, could you help me understand its priority relative to the client proposal and the system update?

1769
01:42:21,770 --> 01:42:23,830
Naming specific existing priorities.

1770
01:42:24,350 --> 01:42:24,590
Okay.

1771
01:42:24,830 --> 01:42:29,420
Asking for help prioritizing the new task against existing named priorities.

1772
01:42:29,440 --> 01:42:29,660
Yes.

1773
01:42:30,140 --> 01:42:33,850
It shifts the burden of prioritization back to the requester.

1774
01:42:34,430 --> 01:42:37,890
It forces a strategic discussion about what truly matters most right now.

1775
01:42:38,370 --> 01:42:42,070
They have to justify bumping something else if they want this done immediately.

1776
01:42:42,140 --> 01:42:43,720
That's a genius move, really.

1777
01:42:43,840 --> 01:42:51,630
You're inviting collaboration on prioritization, but the implied no is loud and clear unless they can make a strong case for moving it up the list.

1778
01:42:51,650 --> 01:42:52,830
It clarifies things quickly.

1779
01:42:53,470 --> 01:42:59,560
And finally, a powerful statement for declining maybe new ideas or side projects that just don't fit the bigger picture right now.

1780
01:42:59,680 --> 01:42:59,960
Yeah.

1781
01:43:00,180 --> 01:43:05,020
While I see the value in this idea, it's not aligned with the strategic priorities set for this quarter.

1782
01:43:05,060 --> 01:43:06,820
Acknowledges the idea's merit first.

1783
01:43:07,120 --> 01:43:08,440
Yes, which is important.

1784
01:43:08,840 --> 01:43:13,390
But then it cites a lack of alignment with broader established strategic goals.

1785
01:43:14,050 --> 01:43:20,900
It positions your refusal not as a dismissal of the idea itself, but as a strategic decision based on the current focus.

1786
01:43:21,530 --> 01:43:26,570
It protects your team's collective focus and resources for what's already been agreed is most important.

1787
01:43:26,820 --> 01:43:33,970
So it's a no that shows you're thinking big picture about the overall strategy, not just about your immediate to-do list.

1788
01:43:34,390 --> 01:43:38,220
That can actually elevate your professional standing, showing you're strategically minded.

1789
01:43:38,270 --> 01:43:39,110
It definitely can.

1790
01:43:39,130 --> 01:43:40,390
It shows you're focused on the right things.

1791
01:43:40,570 --> 01:43:41,550
Okay.

1792
01:43:41,570 --> 01:43:41,830
Wow.

1793
01:43:41,950 --> 01:43:49,380
So we've really taken a deep dive, haven't we, into the surprisingly rich and nuanced language of saying no in business.

1794
01:43:49,420 --> 01:43:51,030
It's more complex than people think.

1795
01:43:51,050 --> 01:43:51,610
It really is.

1796
01:43:51,690 --> 01:43:55,670
From the simple, my plate is full, to the more complex, it falls outside my expertise.

1797
01:43:56,190 --> 01:44:03,750
And all those subtle ways to manage expectations and set boundaries, you really should now have a pretty comprehensive toolkit.

1798
01:44:03,770 --> 01:44:04,630
Hopefully, yes.

1799
01:44:04,610 --> 01:44:09,460
And remember, saying no professionally, it isn't a sign of weakness, is it?

1800
01:44:09,940 --> 01:44:19,310
It's actually a powerful signal of good management, strategic focus, and fundamentally, a deep commitment to quality and effective delivery.

1801
01:44:19,370 --> 01:44:19,990
Absolutely.

1802
01:44:20,490 --> 01:44:27,910
If we connect this back to the bigger picture, mastering these phrases, getting comfortable with them, it allows you to be far more intentional with your yeses.

1803
01:44:28,430 --> 01:44:28,990
Ah, right.

1804
01:44:29,290 --> 01:44:30,710
Making your yes mean more.

1805
01:44:30,890 --> 01:44:31,370
Exactly.

1806
01:44:31,910 --> 01:44:38,350
Which leads to higher quality work, it helps prevent burnout, and ultimately it boosts your overall productivity and impact.

1807
01:44:38,690 --> 01:44:40,410
It's really about respect.

1808
01:44:40,430 --> 01:44:43,470
Respect for your own boundaries and respect for the work itself.

1809
01:44:43,450 --> 01:44:47,820
It empowers you to protect your focus, contribute where you can make the most meaningful impact.

1810
01:44:47,860 --> 01:44:51,300
Yes, benefiting both yourself and your organization in the long run.

1811
01:44:51,660 --> 01:44:52,940
It's a win-win when done right.

1812
01:44:53,210 --> 01:44:55,430
Okay, so here's where it gets really interesting.

1813
01:44:55,790 --> 01:44:57,350
A final thought for everyone listening.

1814
01:44:58,090 --> 01:44:58,570
Think about it.

1815
01:44:59,210 --> 01:45:07,520
Every no you say, especially to something that doesn't truly serve your highest priorities, is actually a profound yes to something else, something more important.

1816
01:45:08,460 --> 01:45:09,230
Creating space.

1817
01:45:09,370 --> 01:45:10,180
Exactly.

1818
01:45:10,220 --> 01:45:16,310
It's not just about what you decline, but what you actively choose to prioritize and pour your valuable energy into.

1819
01:45:16,360 --> 01:45:27,280
So the question to ponder is, what yes are you creating for yourself, maybe for your team or for your most important projects with your next polite, strategic and well-chosen no?

1820
01:45:27,720 --> 01:45:28,660
That's a great way to frame it.

1821
01:45:28,760 --> 01:45:31,100
And it raises one last important question for a reflection.

1822
01:45:31,730 --> 01:45:41,170
In a world that seems to be constantly demanding more and more of our attention, our time, our energy, how might a well-placed strategic no actually be the single most productive thing to do all week?
